/** Original ten-part story: The Museum of Everyday Things. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch52 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries(
            id = "the_museum_of_everyday_things",
            title = "The Museum of Everyday Things",
            tagline = "Students build a small museum display from ordinary objects and the stories their owners choose to share.",
            parts = listOf(
                StoryPart(1, "A Tin Without a Label", listOf(
                    "The school history club was asked to create a display about everyday life. In the archive room, Jide found a biscuit tin filled with ordinary objects: a bus ticket, a button, a wooden spoon, and a small paper fan. There was no label, only a note saying DONATED BY THE COMMUNITY—ASK BEFORE ASSUMING.",
                    "The club's teacher explained that the objects had been collected for an exhibition that never opened. The students could research their history, but they should not invent stories about their owners. Jide suggested inviting people to contribute memories voluntarily, while making clear that an object could be displayed without a personal story."
                )),
                StoryPart(2, "The Button", listOf(
                    "A card inside the tin said the button came from a school uniform, but no name or date was recorded. The club searched old uniform catalogues and found that the school had changed its colours twice. The button's shape matched a design used for several years, but it could not identify a particular student.",
                    "Jide wrote a careful caption: 'A uniform button from an earlier school design; exact owner unknown.' His classmate Sade thought the caption sounded plain. The teacher said accuracy was more valuable than a dramatic guess. They decided the display could invite visitors to share what school uniforms had meant in their own time."
                )),
                StoryPart(3, "The Bus Ticket", listOf(
                    "The ticket showed a route number that no longer existed. The transport museum confirmed that the route had connected the market with the old sports ground. A public timetable helped the students identify the years when the route operated. They still could not know who had used that particular ticket or why.",
                    "The club placed a copy of the old route map beside the ticket. A retired driver visited and explained how the route had changed as the town expanded. He did not claim to know the ticket's owner, but he remembered the crowded match days and the extra buses. The students recorded his account as an interview, with his permission."
                )),
                StoryPart(4, "The Wooden Spoon", listOf(
                    "The spoon was worn smooth along one side. A local craft teacher identified the wood and explained how the handle had likely been shaped by hand. She could not determine its maker or exact age from appearance alone. The club asked whether she would demonstrate traditional carving techniques using a separate piece of wood.",
                    "The teacher agreed and brought examples from her own workshop. She explained how tools and methods had changed, and why an object should not be dated without evidence. Jide added a caption describing the spoon's material and visible wear, with a note that its original use was not confirmed."
                )),
                StoryPart(5, "The Paper Fan", listOf(
                    "The fan had a small painted pattern and a repaired fold. The museum's textile specialist recognised the style as common in several regions, so it could not be linked to one place without more information. The students photographed it for the catalogue and stored it in a protective sleeve.",
                    "A visitor to the archive recognised the pattern from a fan used at community celebrations. She offered to share a general memory of seeing similar fans at outdoor events, but she did not claim the object was hers. The club included her recollection as a separate oral-history note, clearly labelled as a personal memory rather than proof of the fan's origin."
                )),
                StoryPart(6, "The Owners' Invitation", listOf(
                    "The club created a notice asking residents to share memories of everyday objects. It said contributions were optional, could be anonymous, and would be reviewed before display. People could also donate an object without telling a personal story. The school provided a contact address and a consent form explaining how recordings and photographs might be used.",
                    "Several residents responded. One offered a recipe card, another brought a repaired school bag, and a third shared a photograph of a public market. The club accepted only items that could be stored safely and documented properly. The teacher checked that donors understood whether an item would be returned or kept by the school."
                )),
                StoryPart(7, "A Story with Permission", listOf(
                    "A resident named Mrs Bello donated a small hand-sewn pencil case and told the club how she had made similar cases for a school fundraiser. She approved a short caption and asked that her family details remain private. The students wrote about the fundraiser and the craft, not about information she had chosen to keep to herself.",
                    "Another donor changed their mind after seeing the draft display. The club returned the item promptly and removed its photograph from the exhibit files. Jide realised that permission was not a one-time box to tick; the team had to respect changes and keep accurate records of what could be shown."
                )),
                StoryPart(8, "Putting the Display Together", listOf(
                    "The club arranged the objects by theme rather than by guessing their age. A section about travel included the bus ticket and route map. A section about making and mending included the spoon, pencil case, and button. The paper fan sat beside the oral-history note, with a clear distinction between what was known about the object and what a visitor remembered.",
                    "The museum specialist reviewed the labels and suggested larger text and a simple glossary. The students added dates only where a source supported them. They also included a note explaining that ordinary objects can have many stories, and that the display showed only the information contributors had chosen to share."
                )),
                StoryPart(9, "Opening the Museum", listOf(
                    "Families visited the school hall and recognised familiar objects from their own homes. Some left written memories, while others simply looked around. Mrs Bello attended and approved the final caption. The retired driver answered questions about the old route, and the craft teacher demonstrated a safe paper-folding activity for younger visitors.",
                    "The club placed a comment box beside the exit. It asked visitors what everyday object they would preserve and why, without requesting names. The responses showed that people valued practical things: a lunch container, a notebook, a repaired toy, a key ring. Jide saw that history was not limited to famous events or expensive possessions."
                )),
                StoryPart(10, "The Next Tin", listOf(
                    "After the exhibition, the objects were returned to the archive or their owners according to the agreed records. The club created a catalogue with source notes, permissions, and storage instructions. The teacher arranged for next year's students to review the display and add new contributions only after checking consent and condition.",
                    "Jide placed a new empty tin on the shelf with a label: EVERYDAY THINGS—FUTURE STORIES. It was not a request to fill it quickly. It was an invitation to notice what people use, repair, remember, and choose to share. The museum had begun with ordinary objects, but its real collection was the care taken to tell each story honestly."
                ))
            )
        )
    )
}
